1
00:00:11,448 --> 00:00:16,072
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this is another talk by Ajahn Chah called Path of Peace.

2
00:00:16,192 --> 00:00:19,014
[SPEAKER_01]: This is just a few paragraphs from it that I found interesting.

3
00:00:20,475 --> 00:00:25,059
[SPEAKER_01]: In this talk, Ajahn Chah gives a complete outline of the practice.

4
00:00:29,042 --> 00:00:32,985
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he begins... well, it's about in the middle of the talk that I begin from.

5
00:00:34,487 --> 00:00:39,310
[SPEAKER_01]: At a certain point in the practice, you see that it is the mind which gives orders to the body.

6
00:00:40,777 --> 00:00:43,600
[SPEAKER_01]: The body has to depend on the mind before it can function.

7
00:00:44,741 --> 00:00:53,050
[SPEAKER_01]: However, the mind itself is constantly subject to different objects contacting and conditioning it, before it can have any effect on the body.

8
00:00:54,688 --> 00:01:16,571
[SPEAKER_01]: As you continue to turn attention inwards and reflect on the dhamma, the wisdom faculty gradually matures, and eventually you are left contemplating the mind and mind-objects, which means that you start to experience the body, rūpa-dhamma, as ārūpa-dhamma, formless.

9
00:01:19,617 --> 00:01:26,059
[SPEAKER_01]: Through your insight, you are no longer uncertain in your understanding of the body and the way it is.

10
00:01:26,119 --> 00:01:36,241
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind experiences the body's physical characteristics as ārūpa dhamma, or formless object, which come into contact with the mind.

11
00:01:37,821 --> 00:01:44,003
[SPEAKER_01]: Ultimately, you are contemplating just the mind and mind objects, those objects which come into your consciousness.

12
00:01:46,252 --> 00:01:55,156
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, examining the true nature of the mind, you can observe that in its natural state, it has no preoccupations or issues prevailing upon it.

13
00:01:56,077 --> 00:01:59,879
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like a piece of cloth or a flag that has been tied to the end of a pole.

14
00:02:00,879 --> 00:02:06,142
[SPEAKER_01]: As long as it's on its own and undisturbed, nothing will happen to it.

15
00:02:07,122 --> 00:02:09,045
[SPEAKER_01]: A leaf on a tree is another example.

16
00:02:10,147 --> 00:02:12,910
[SPEAKER_01]: Ordinarily, it remains quiet and unperturbed.

17
00:02:14,072 --> 00:02:18,999
[SPEAKER_01]: If it moves or flutters, this must be due to the wind, an external force.

18
00:02:20,437 --> 00:02:22,418
[SPEAKER_01]: Normally, nothing much happens to leaves.

19
00:02:22,979 --> 00:02:23,759
[SPEAKER_01]: They remain still.

20
00:02:24,820 --> 00:02:27,622
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't go looking to get involved with anything or anybody.

21
00:02:28,722 --> 00:02:35,707
[SPEAKER_01]: When they start to move, it must be due to the influence of something external, such as the wind, which makes them swing back and forth.

22
00:02:36,907 --> 00:02:37,788
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a natural state.

23
00:02:38,528 --> 00:02:39,429
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind is the same.

24
00:02:40,590 --> 00:02:40,810
[SPEAKER_01]: In it,

25
00:02:41,514 --> 00:02:46,415
[SPEAKER_01]: There exists no loving or hating, nor does it seek to blame other people.

26
00:02:47,375 --> 00:02:53,197
[SPEAKER_01]: It is independent, existing in a state of purity that is truly clear, radiant and untarnished.

27
00:02:53,997 --> 00:02:57,918
[SPEAKER_01]: In its pure state, the mind is peaceful, without happiness or suffering.

28
00:02:59,018 --> 00:03:01,898
[SPEAKER_01]: Indeed, not experiencing any feeling at all.

29
00:03:02,519 --> 00:03:04,439
[SPEAKER_01]: This is the true state of the mind.

30
00:03:08,092 --> 00:03:09,213
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

31
00:03:09,473 --> 00:03:16,459
[SPEAKER_02]: No, it's nothing other than saying you start seeing things arise as phenomena, appear as phenomena, which they already are.

32
00:03:17,059 --> 00:03:24,525
[SPEAKER_02]: Because phenomena, dhamma, is the object of the mind, of māna, as a sense.

33
00:03:25,706 --> 00:03:37,655
[SPEAKER_02]: And most people in their day-to-day life, you don't even see that, because you're too absorbed with sense world, and usually that then results in all the views as well.

34
00:03:38,646 --> 00:03:49,094
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is not... That's why it's so common to have the assumption of the external public world that we inhabit, the world that is independent of our experience, the material world.

35
00:03:49,875 --> 00:03:59,802
[SPEAKER_02]: But you fail to see that even your assumption of material, it's actually a phenomena risen on the level of your thought, on the level of the dhamma, on the level of the image in your mind.

36
00:04:01,644 --> 00:04:07,108
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's usually how people go about in their day-to-day lives, like not even seeing.

37
00:04:10,339 --> 00:04:14,180
[SPEAKER_02]: Not even seeing the mind, the field where these phenomena appear.

38
00:04:15,061 --> 00:04:17,162
[SPEAKER_02]: So, there are no signs of it, no recognition of it.

39
00:04:17,682 --> 00:04:22,504
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then everything revolves around assumptions of material world, interpretations based on that.

40
00:04:22,824 --> 00:04:27,205
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's all like, again, in itself, it's still on the level of phenomena.

41
00:04:28,326 --> 00:04:32,687
[SPEAKER_02]: But the only difference is a person is completely unaware of it, or a person can be aware of it.

42
00:04:33,188 --> 00:04:37,049
[SPEAKER_02]: So, once you start recognizing that your own body

43
00:04:37,617 --> 00:04:47,447
[SPEAKER_02]: No matter how material it is, how material it might feel, it still can only be known as such, because it has arisen on the level of that mind.

44
00:04:49,048 --> 00:04:58,377
[SPEAKER_02]: And in that sense, you realize, oh, yeah, this is, in a way, has nothing to do with this matter that I'm thinking of, this matter that I assume.

45
00:04:58,938 --> 00:04:59,799
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the opposite way.

46
00:05:01,032 --> 00:05:08,759
[SPEAKER_02]: The matter that I think of and assume is only intelligible because the thing is still there on the level of that mind as a phenomenon.

47
00:05:09,780 --> 00:05:14,124
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's what Ajahn Chah referred to as ārūpa, like non-material.

48
00:05:15,686 --> 00:05:27,076
[SPEAKER_02]: But even a non-material, even a material thing is known only as material thing because that phenomenon has arisen on the level of your mind, which is non-material.

49
00:05:27,696 --> 00:05:32,237
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why he meant, like, the mind is the one that governs and that perceives these things structurally.

50
00:05:35,418 --> 00:05:42,420
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in other words, nobody, like, like, a person now might start thinking, oh, so, the matter, I must find the immaterial or something like that.

51
00:05:43,120 --> 00:05:53,122
[SPEAKER_02]: It's, it's actually, eh, the arūpa that Ajahn Chah refers to, like, the phenomenal nature of things, it's within the material that you're perceiving.

52
00:05:53,162 --> 00:05:55,083
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not that you must abandon or deny.

53
00:05:55,839 --> 00:05:59,242
[SPEAKER_02]: Or get rid of the material, or stop thinking it, in order to see the material.

54
00:05:59,322 --> 00:05:59,482
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

55
00:05:59,762 --> 00:06:09,329
[SPEAKER_02]: You just have to discern it properly, whereby you know that a recent experience of material body, right here, right now, is an image in your mind, already.

56
00:06:10,530 --> 00:06:14,774
[SPEAKER_02]: So, and that's like these two tiers of existence, so to speak, two domains.

57
00:06:17,055 --> 00:06:20,118
[SPEAKER_02]: Simultaneous presence of the material domain and the mental domain.

58
00:06:20,498 --> 00:06:24,101
[SPEAKER_02]: Material is inconceivable without a mental designation of it.

59
00:06:24,655 --> 00:06:27,256
[SPEAKER_02]: Without the mental phenomena being there simultaneously present.

60
00:06:27,676 --> 00:06:39,199
[SPEAKER_02]: But, in the same manner, there would be nothing present as a phenomena on the level of mental domain, if the actual physical rūpa is not there, still alive.

61
00:06:41,040 --> 00:06:45,521
[SPEAKER_02]: So, nāmarūpa determines the viññāṇa, viññāṇa determines the nāmarūpa, to the same extent.

62
00:06:46,081 --> 00:06:46,761
[SPEAKER_02]: Like two sticks.

63
00:06:46,781 --> 00:06:50,542
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you can't separate, you can't investigate them independent of each other.

64
00:06:50,722 --> 00:06:51,563
[SPEAKER_02]: One implies the other.

65
00:06:51,663 --> 00:06:52,563
[SPEAKER_02]: That's just how it works.

66
00:06:53,708 --> 00:07:09,046
[SPEAKER_02]: But in practical terms, the way the experience proliferates, with sense... lack of sensory strain and sensuality in the views, you drift away from that phenomenal side of things that's simultaneously there.

67
00:07:09,106 --> 00:07:10,328
[SPEAKER_02]: You drift away from your mind.

68
00:07:11,160 --> 00:07:15,323
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why Buddha said it's hard to see the mind correctly for what it is.

69
00:07:15,363 --> 00:07:26,092
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why it's a it's a prerequisite for sotāpatti, seeing the signs of your mind, seeing the domain of the phenomenal, phenomenological, whatever you want to call it.

70
00:07:26,172 --> 00:07:28,594
[SPEAKER_02]: Because for most people that's completely overseen.

71
00:07:29,014 --> 00:07:29,955
[SPEAKER_02]: Just, it's just...

72
00:07:30,696 --> 00:07:31,496
[SPEAKER_01]: Into the senses.

73
00:07:31,856 --> 00:07:35,099
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, usually the entire attention gets absorbed.

74
00:07:35,439 --> 00:07:38,642
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if you don't necessarily proliferate or you're like wild, unrestrained.

75
00:07:38,662 --> 00:07:39,803
[SPEAKER_02]: No, I think just naturally,

76
00:07:40,627 --> 00:07:56,574
[SPEAKER_02]: Not discerning your mind means automatically over-discerning that which comes from your senses, which then influence all the views that you have on account of it, which is the public, material world, independent of my experience, science, scientific sort of measuring data as the objective value.

77
00:07:56,894 --> 00:08:04,557
[SPEAKER_02]: Again, independent of my experience, failing to see that you cannot even conceive those things unless they are part of your... unless they are your experience.

78
00:08:05,037 --> 00:08:06,758
[SPEAKER_01]: So, thinking, when I die,

79
00:08:07,188 --> 00:08:08,289
[SPEAKER_01]: This world will continue.

80
00:08:08,409 --> 00:08:09,230
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly, exactly.

81
00:08:09,250 --> 00:08:15,234
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's like the the fundamental, two fundamental, like, all the wrong views can be boiled down to the two fundamental points.

82
00:08:16,015 --> 00:08:20,739
[SPEAKER_02]: When I die, the world will continue, or when I die, I will continue, not the world.

83
00:08:21,640 --> 00:08:32,428
[SPEAKER_02]: Either way, it's this external projection of your experience as a whole, which is wrong, not because of some higher authority told you to, to regard it as wrong.

84
00:08:32,448 --> 00:08:34,710
[SPEAKER_02]: It's wrong because it's a contradiction in terms.

85
00:08:37,692 --> 00:08:43,093
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, how can you even know something external of your experience, if that's not already experienced?

86
00:08:43,273 --> 00:08:45,294
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means, then, it's not external of your experience.

87
00:08:46,354 --> 00:08:57,736
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's your experience, parts of it present themselves through your own ignorance, well, based on your own ignorance, present themselves as they're independent of your experience, but you're experiencing it.

88
00:08:57,776 --> 00:08:58,936
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's a contradiction in terms.

89
00:08:59,176 --> 00:09:02,717
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why ātavāda is one of the first contradictions to go, when you get the right view.

90
00:09:03,497 --> 00:09:07,458
[SPEAKER_02]: The external, the assumption of the external sense of self, independent of this experience.

91
00:09:08,815 --> 00:09:16,082
[SPEAKER_02]: And that will go when you realize that no matter how external it might feel, it's still experienced, which means it's still internal in that manner.

92
00:09:17,243 --> 00:09:28,232
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it doesn't matter how material, how objective it is, the notion of objectivity, notion of materiality, it's on the level of the phenomenon persisting in your mind that gives it its meaning.

93
00:09:28,292 --> 00:09:30,875
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why things are significant and determined by the mind.

94
00:09:30,895 --> 00:09:33,137
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why the mind is the forerunner, as the Buddha would say.

95
00:09:34,019 --> 00:09:36,740
[SPEAKER_02]: In the dhammapada, the forerunner of all things.

96
00:09:37,140 --> 00:09:43,142
[SPEAKER_02]: Without the mind giving its determination to these things, it just... well, it would be inconceivable, there would be no experience.

97
00:09:45,023 --> 00:09:58,407
[SPEAKER_02]: But now that you say that it's all in the mind, that's not true either, because that mind wouldn't be there as a... like mirroring the phenomena, if the matter is not there to be mirrored in the first place.

98
00:09:58,527 --> 00:10:00,188
[SPEAKER_02]: If the four great elements are not there.

99
00:10:01,063 --> 00:10:05,088
[SPEAKER_01]: And it shows that inaccessibility of that matter to you.

100
00:10:05,849 --> 00:10:06,169
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

101
00:10:06,189 --> 00:10:07,051
[SPEAKER_01]: How inaccessible it is.

102
00:10:07,251 --> 00:10:12,537
[SPEAKER_02]: The only way you can access it is the indirect experience of it, which is not it.

103
00:10:13,158 --> 00:10:13,999
[SPEAKER_02]: It's because of it.

104
00:10:14,680 --> 00:10:15,681
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the anicca.

105
00:10:16,400 --> 00:10:18,261
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, exactly, exactly, exactly, exactly.

106
00:10:18,361 --> 00:10:20,261
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why the Mūla Pariyāra Sutta, that conceiving.

107
00:10:20,301 --> 00:10:22,302
[SPEAKER_02]: He conceives in matter, apart from matter.

108
00:10:23,842 --> 00:10:25,143
[SPEAKER_02]: Matter is mine and so on.

109
00:10:25,443 --> 00:10:34,985
[SPEAKER_02]: He develops all these attitudes towards that, which is matter, failing to see that he can only experience his experience of matter, not the matter.

110
00:10:36,326 --> 00:10:38,986
[SPEAKER_02]: So, his perception can only perceive perception.

111
00:10:39,587 --> 00:10:41,167
[SPEAKER_02]: His feeling can only feel the feeling.

112
00:10:41,227 --> 00:10:43,228
[SPEAKER_02]: Intentions can only intend...

113
00:10:43,608 --> 00:10:44,108
[SPEAKER_02]: Intentions.

114
00:10:45,672 --> 00:10:58,022
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you are not perce... because assuming that you are perceiving that the genuine rūpa, means you are actually accessing that external world of the four Mahābhūtas, the four great elements, and that's inconceivable.

115
00:10:58,762 --> 00:11:04,747
[SPEAKER_02]: Hence, the slightest of those assumptions, as described in Mūla Parāsuta, means there is a conceiving of I am.

116
00:11:04,927 --> 00:11:10,672
[SPEAKER_02]: There is a conceiving of a separate entity that's independent and objective from the experience as a whole.

117
00:11:11,898 --> 00:11:18,220
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why the Buddha referred to the four great elements, they cease to find footing.

118
00:11:18,800 --> 00:11:20,541
[SPEAKER_02]: They don't cease to be wherever they are.

119
00:11:21,881 --> 00:11:24,422
[SPEAKER_02]: But they stop finding footing in your experience.

120
00:11:25,222 --> 00:11:33,945
[SPEAKER_02]: As in, you stop conflating the perception that has arisen on account of the four great elements being there, with the perception of the actual four great elements.

121
00:11:34,325 --> 00:11:37,606
[SPEAKER_02]: But see, now when you think, oh, so then the four great elements are something different.

122
00:11:37,686 --> 00:11:39,947
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that's also your perception on the level of your thought.

123
00:11:40,852 --> 00:11:48,774
[SPEAKER_02]: So, by no means of grasping, by your thought, by your intentions, can you actually ever enter the domain of the four great elements.

124
00:11:48,794 --> 00:11:51,914
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you realize all you have to do is stop misconceiving it.

125
00:11:52,334 --> 00:11:54,415
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how it will stop finding the footing.

126
00:11:54,435 --> 00:11:56,995
[SPEAKER_02]: Not by finding it where it is and removing it and so on.

127
00:11:57,495 --> 00:12:00,856
[SPEAKER_02]: Just stop making a mistake of thinking that you can relate to it.

128
00:12:01,676 --> 00:12:06,357
[SPEAKER_02]: And you will keep making the mistake of relating to it for as long as you hold your sense of self dearly.

129
00:12:07,613 --> 00:12:14,894
[SPEAKER_02]: Because the relations with the world is the direct result and also direct fuel for the sense of self.

130
00:12:17,255 --> 00:12:26,517
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you're willing to let go of that sense of self, you will then have no reason to keep maintaining this gratuitous assumption of the world external to you.

131
00:12:28,457 --> 00:12:33,258
[SPEAKER_02]: Because the only reason you do that maintenance is because that's how you maintain your sense of self.

132
00:12:39,912 --> 00:12:43,435
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what about, as Ajahn Chah is saying, the state of the mind?

133
00:12:44,175 --> 00:12:58,246
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you start recognizing that no amount of materiality, objectivity, can be independent of the basis of... can be, like, found elsewhere, except on the basis of the mind, you realize that the mind is the gateway.

134
00:12:59,167 --> 00:13:07,954
[SPEAKER_02]: Doesn't matter what comes your way through your senses, good or bad, threatening or agreeable and friendly, the mind is the basis.

135
00:13:08,902 --> 00:13:14,426
[SPEAKER_02]: And, in itself, on that basis of the mind, things are quite indifferent.

136
00:13:16,567 --> 00:13:37,282
[SPEAKER_02]: It's your own attitude, then, towards what comes through the mind, by not seeing that you want to deal with it, prevent it, want more of it, indulge in sensuality, indulge in... engage in ill-will, because you don't see that... Well, you don't need to go and chase these things out there, because even the assumption of out there, it's only been known as such on the level of the phenomenon of your mind.

137
00:13:37,999 --> 00:13:41,401
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means, what if I just know it as a persisting, enduring phenomenon right here, right now?

138
00:13:41,782 --> 00:13:42,682
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't need to go anywhere.

139
00:13:43,343 --> 00:13:44,684
[SPEAKER_02]: I just stay with this framework.

140
00:13:46,405 --> 00:13:57,052
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, there will be no overly delighting or trying to deny to get rid of it, which means equanimity will be like a natural result.

141
00:13:57,092 --> 00:13:59,834
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's what is there, in a way, without you...

142
00:14:00,374 --> 00:14:00,815
[SPEAKER_02]: Absolutely.

143
00:14:00,895 --> 00:14:03,577
[SPEAKER_02]: The mind and the body are there without you.

144
00:14:03,697 --> 00:14:05,118
[SPEAKER_02]: They don't need your sense of self.

145
00:14:07,027 --> 00:14:12,870
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why you can develop equanimity, because things in themselves are equanimous.

146
00:14:13,090 --> 00:14:14,051
[SPEAKER_02]: They're indifferent to you.

147
00:14:14,671 --> 00:14:22,796
[SPEAKER_02]: It's your own passion and confusion, and passion that comes out of that confusion, that confuses that whole thing.

148
00:14:23,276 --> 00:14:30,220
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you stop, you yourself, if you stop fueling that passion, confusion disappears, which means then equanimity is restored.

149
00:14:30,941 --> 00:14:34,783
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, yeah, all you have is things that have arisen and they persist, and that's it.

150
00:14:36,130 --> 00:14:37,070
[SPEAKER_02]: It has nothing to do with you.

151
00:14:38,550 --> 00:14:41,291
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'll continue with Ajahn Chah's talk, Path of Peace.

152
00:14:42,351 --> 00:14:42,951
[SPEAKER_01]: And he continues.

153
00:14:43,991 --> 00:14:50,913
[SPEAKER_01]: The purpose of practice, then, is to seek inwardly, searching and investigating until you reach the original mind.

154
00:14:52,333 --> 00:14:54,413
[SPEAKER_01]: The original mind is also known as the pure mind.

155
00:14:54,853 --> 00:14:57,154
[SPEAKER_01]: The pure mind is the mind without attachment.

156
00:14:58,674 --> 00:14:59,634
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what I just said.

157
00:14:59,714 --> 00:15:00,714
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, exactly.

158
00:15:01,654 --> 00:15:05,495
[SPEAKER_02]: You find the phenomenon there, that you realize phenomenon, the way they have arisen.

159
00:15:06,831 --> 00:15:15,075
[SPEAKER_02]: In that mind, are already indifferent, are already non-polluted by passion and lust, and it can't really be polluted.

160
00:15:15,495 --> 00:15:27,081
[SPEAKER_02]: Your actions can be polluted by desire and lust, but the persisting phenomena, it's the way it has arisen, which means it's impenetrable to your... your assumptions, your cravings.

161
00:15:27,861 --> 00:15:28,561
[SPEAKER_01]: Your attachments.

162
00:15:28,641 --> 00:15:29,382
[SPEAKER_02]: Your attachments.

163
00:15:29,482 --> 00:15:34,424
[SPEAKER_02]: It's your attachment that... that's why it needs constant maintenance, because it can never really get settled in these things that you're

164
00:15:34,898 --> 00:15:36,859
[SPEAKER_02]: Attached to, or trying to get rid of, or whatever.

165
00:15:37,359 --> 00:15:38,940
[SPEAKER_02]: It's only an attitude in regard to it.

166
00:15:39,380 --> 00:15:41,702
[SPEAKER_02]: But thing in itself remains completely indifferent.

167
00:15:42,162 --> 00:15:50,166
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you recognize that, that the true indifference, true equanimity, comes from the things, the way they have arisen, and you have no say in it, even if you want to have a say.

168
00:15:50,266 --> 00:15:51,366
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the original state.

169
00:15:51,487 --> 00:15:51,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

170
00:15:51,867 --> 00:15:54,288
[SPEAKER_02]: Then you realize, oh, things were always like this in a way.

171
00:15:54,448 --> 00:15:56,389
[SPEAKER_02]: It was me not knowing that they were this way.

172
00:15:56,889 --> 00:15:58,510
[SPEAKER_02]: I kept assuming them to be different.

173
00:16:00,891 --> 00:16:04,053
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the pure mind is the mind without attachment.

174
00:16:04,578 --> 00:16:06,879
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't get affected by mind-objects.

175
00:16:08,079 --> 00:16:13,481
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, it doesn't chase after the different kinds of pleasant and unpleasant mind-objects.

176
00:16:13,521 --> 00:16:20,643
[SPEAKER_01]: Rather, the mind is in a state of continuous knowing and wakefulness, thoroughly mindful of all it's experiencing.

177
00:16:21,844 --> 00:16:28,166
[SPEAKER_01]: When the mind is like this, no pleasant or unpleasant mind-objects it experiences will be able to disturb it.

178
00:16:29,046 --> 00:16:30,667
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind doesn't become anything.

179
00:16:31,747 --> 00:16:34,148
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, nothing can shake it.

180
00:16:34,188 --> 00:16:34,408
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

181
00:16:35,072 --> 00:16:36,113
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is awareness.

182
00:16:37,293 --> 00:16:39,034
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind knows itself as pure.

183
00:16:39,954 --> 00:16:42,136
[SPEAKER_01]: It has evolved its own true independence.

184
00:16:42,776 --> 00:16:44,157
[SPEAKER_01]: It has reached its original state.

185
00:16:45,197 --> 00:16:48,019
[SPEAKER_01]: How is it able to bring this original state into existence?

186
00:16:49,039 --> 00:17:00,945
[SPEAKER_01]: Through the faculty of mindfulness, wisely reflecting and seeing that all things are merely conditions arising out of the influence of elements, without any individual being controlling them.

187
00:17:01,105 --> 00:17:04,587
[SPEAKER_02]: The mind gives the meaning, gives the significance, simultaneously,

188
00:17:05,600 --> 00:17:09,643
[SPEAKER_02]: To the present material domain.

189
00:17:11,123 --> 00:17:15,706
[SPEAKER_02]: But without the material domain, there would be nothing manifesting in the mind.

190
00:17:17,207 --> 00:17:20,009
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's the two sticks holding each other.

191
00:17:20,049 --> 00:17:22,130
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the dyad, as the Buddha referred to.

192
00:17:22,770 --> 00:17:25,532
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the experience as a whole.

193
00:17:25,652 --> 00:17:26,613
[SPEAKER_02]: Back in front.

194
00:17:26,673 --> 00:17:26,993
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

195
00:17:27,093 --> 00:17:27,653
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the dyad.

196
00:17:27,693 --> 00:17:31,135
[SPEAKER_02]: Wherever you look, it's within these two bases that are mutually determined.

197
00:17:31,816 --> 00:17:33,617
[SPEAKER_02]: So, there is no room for your sense of self.

198
00:17:35,532 --> 00:17:37,073
[SPEAKER_02]: for your ownership, for your mastery.

199
00:17:37,513 --> 00:17:46,180
[SPEAKER_02]: Or rather, your sense of ownership, as it is now, it's within that, which means it's determined by that basis, independent of your sense of self.

200
00:17:46,720 --> 00:17:54,706
[SPEAKER_02]: And the sense of self, that's not in your own control, well, that's not yourself then, isn't it?

201
00:17:54,766 --> 00:18:00,430
[SPEAKER_02]: Because sense of self, it's implicit, implicitly, basically, declares ownership

202
00:18:01,036 --> 00:18:02,617
[SPEAKER_02]: mastery over experience.

203
00:18:02,797 --> 00:18:05,199
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why it's my self, my own self.

204
00:18:05,699 --> 00:18:09,862
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you realize that your own self depends upon this basis that you have no say in.

205
00:18:11,143 --> 00:18:13,044
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's how your own self is not yours.

206
00:18:14,305 --> 00:18:21,129
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you realize the basis that's not myself, that cannot be myself, determines this sense of self that's because of that not myself.

207
00:18:21,589 --> 00:18:27,673
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you actually learn how to perceive not-self with the not-self.

208
00:18:27,973 --> 00:18:30,135
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's what the Buddha was talking about in those different suttas.

209
00:18:31,458 --> 00:18:34,801
[SPEAKER_02]: So, when Ajahn Chah talks about the original mind, again, you can mystify that.

210
00:18:34,921 --> 00:18:38,744
[SPEAKER_02]: You can think, oh, it's this pure, bright mind that you just have to tap into.

211
00:18:39,244 --> 00:18:39,384
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

212
00:18:39,664 --> 00:18:44,068
[SPEAKER_02]: The original mind is right in front of you, where your thoughts are, where these appearances are.

213
00:18:44,828 --> 00:18:48,271
[SPEAKER_02]: The way things arise, you realize they have arisen on their own, to that extent.

214
00:18:48,771 --> 00:18:49,672
[SPEAKER_02]: And you're not saying that.

215
00:18:50,293 --> 00:18:52,114
[SPEAKER_02]: And that is that originality of it.

216
00:18:52,754 --> 00:18:56,918
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not your mastery, your creation of those same things.

217
00:18:57,658 --> 00:19:01,201
[SPEAKER_02]: You realize you can only appropriate things to be mind, because they were given beforehand.

218
00:19:01,722 --> 00:19:03,903
[SPEAKER_02]: They've arisen on their own, so I appropriate them.

219
00:19:04,003 --> 00:19:06,984
[SPEAKER_02]: You realize you can't even create anything, in that sense.

220
00:19:07,944 --> 00:19:09,065
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's not like a delay.

221
00:19:09,125 --> 00:19:11,066
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not things have arisen, then you don't see them.

222
00:19:11,486 --> 00:19:16,468
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a simultaneous presence of these things enduring and your ownership of that endurance.

223
00:19:17,168 --> 00:19:19,509
[SPEAKER_02]: But you want to realize that that endurance cannot be owned.

224
00:19:19,849 --> 00:19:21,289
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how you stop owning things.

225
00:19:21,389 --> 00:19:25,111
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't stop owning things by trying to destroy them, get rid of them and say no to everything.

226
00:19:27,252 --> 00:19:30,933
[SPEAKER_02]: You can stop owning them by realizing that your ownership cannot belong to you.

227
00:19:31,331 --> 00:19:34,293
[SPEAKER_02]: Hence, it's not the ultimate ownership.

228
00:19:34,313 --> 00:19:35,154
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's an assumption.

229
00:19:35,735 --> 00:19:36,115
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

230
00:19:36,575 --> 00:19:46,323
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the original, the pure mind, seeing how, like, as Ajahn Chah said, like, there is, there is no, there is no room for anyone there, in a way, means exactly that.

231
00:19:46,844 --> 00:20:00,355
[SPEAKER_02]: Inasmuch the mind gives the significance and recognition, allows matter to manifest on its basis, in that, to that same extent, without that matter, there would be nothing for mind to be discerned by.

232
00:20:02,364 --> 00:20:07,948
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the matter is the measure of the extent of the mind, and the mind determines the extent of the appeared matter.

233
00:20:09,689 --> 00:20:12,671
[SPEAKER_02]: And, whichever way you look, it's going to be determined by the other.

234
00:20:15,373 --> 00:20:20,177
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I am, independent of this, becomes inconceivable to even assume.

235
00:20:22,338 --> 00:20:27,522
[SPEAKER_02]: But, in order to see this correctly, a person has to stop just focusing on things in front of you.

236
00:20:28,267 --> 00:20:33,672
[SPEAKER_02]: Because these two levels I talk about, that Ajahn Chah talks about, they're not in front of you as two objects.

237
00:20:34,232 --> 00:20:35,513
[SPEAKER_02]: Only one can be in front of you.

238
00:20:35,954 --> 00:20:38,916
[SPEAKER_02]: The other one is always from behind, from where you look.

239
00:20:39,717 --> 00:20:46,163
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's when we spoke about in other talks about the peripheral, like learning how to see things peripherally without directly looking at them.

240
00:20:46,942 --> 00:20:47,983
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's where the mind is.

241
00:20:48,143 --> 00:20:49,044
[SPEAKER_02]: That's where phenomena are.

242
00:20:49,204 --> 00:20:51,527
[SPEAKER_02]: What you see in front of you is the objects of your senses.

243
00:20:51,567 --> 00:20:58,995
[SPEAKER_02]: That's where people are naturally, with the grain, automatically absorbed with the world and senses and chasing pleasures.

244
00:20:59,456 --> 00:21:01,798
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it takes actually effort to learn how to...

245
00:21:03,279 --> 00:21:04,680
[SPEAKER_02]: See the context behind it.

246
00:21:04,980 --> 00:21:09,882
[SPEAKER_02]: How to develop that peripheral vision, without needing to turn away and look at it, because it won't be peripheral.

247
00:21:10,342 --> 00:21:13,584
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, I'm looking at you now, and I have all these things peripheral to me.

248
00:21:14,204 --> 00:21:16,885
[SPEAKER_02]: And they will remain peripheral, if I keep looking at you.

249
00:21:17,765 --> 00:21:20,326
[SPEAKER_02]: But if I start looking at that, well, that's not peripheral anymore.

250
00:21:21,027 --> 00:21:23,007
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, that's the actual right in front of me.

251
00:21:23,988 --> 00:21:29,150
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the point that you want to... that you must keep in mind, if you try to discern what Ajahn Chah is describing here.

252
00:21:29,726 --> 00:21:36,488
[SPEAKER_02]: That original mind, that ārūpa, it's basically, rūpa is what you're staring, ārūpa would be everything around it.

253
00:21:37,428 --> 00:21:43,930
[SPEAKER_02]: If you look at it one day, that's rūpa, because, yeah, there is rūpa there, that gives it its existence, but then everything else is a rūpa.

254
00:21:44,210 --> 00:21:46,330
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you want to learn how to see a rūpa as a rūpa.

255
00:21:46,850 --> 00:21:48,611
[SPEAKER_02]: You want to see the peripheral as peripheral.

256
00:21:49,551 --> 00:21:56,313
[SPEAKER_02]: By the way, a rūpa and rūpa are not quite used in this sense in the suttas, but Ajahn Chah used it on a practical level and there

257
00:22:11,120 --> 00:22:12,741
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'll continue with the talk.

258
00:22:14,522 --> 00:22:19,245
[SPEAKER_01]: This is how it is with the happiness and suffering we experience.

259
00:22:19,925 --> 00:22:23,167
[SPEAKER_01]: When these mental states arise, they are just happiness and suffering.

260
00:22:24,208 --> 00:22:25,828
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no owner of the happiness.

261
00:22:26,509 --> 00:22:28,450
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind is not the owner of the suffering.

262
00:22:29,631 --> 00:22:31,392
[SPEAKER_01]: Mental states do not belong to the mind.

263
00:22:32,332 --> 00:22:33,333
[SPEAKER_01]: Look at it for yourself.

264
00:22:34,478 --> 00:22:36,539
[SPEAKER_01]: In reality, these are not affairs of the mind.

265
00:22:37,039 --> 00:22:38,440
[SPEAKER_01]: They are separate and distinct.

266
00:22:39,040 --> 00:22:40,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Happiness is just a state of happiness.

267
00:22:41,322 --> 00:22:43,063
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering is just a state of suffering.

268
00:22:43,263 --> 00:22:50,206
[SPEAKER_02]: Any, any phenomena is a phenomena in itself, which means, to that extent, it's independent of you.

269
00:22:50,947 --> 00:22:52,127
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why he knows the mind.

270
00:22:53,248 --> 00:22:54,849
[SPEAKER_02]: Affected with lust is mind, affected with lust.

271
00:22:54,869 --> 00:22:56,510
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not like me affected with lust.

272
00:22:57,130 --> 00:22:59,191
[SPEAKER_02]: There is lust present, there is non-lust present.

273
00:23:00,492 --> 00:23:02,693
[SPEAKER_02]: There is happiness present, there is sadness present.

274
00:23:03,385 --> 00:23:08,168
[SPEAKER_02]: It's enduring in as much sights are enduring, sounds are enduring, and so on.

275
00:23:09,309 --> 00:23:14,232
[SPEAKER_02]: Anything that manifests, that is its nature to be manifested.

276
00:23:14,252 --> 00:23:14,592
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

277
00:23:14,952 --> 00:23:20,376
[SPEAKER_02]: So, even if it's a weird, ambiguous thought, it is real as such, as an experience of an ambiguous thought.

278
00:23:21,897 --> 00:23:31,683
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's our own kind of expectation of concrete, which is fueled by that assumption of material, public concreteness, so to speak, the world independent of me.

279
00:23:33,420 --> 00:23:36,461
[SPEAKER_02]: That prevents you from seeing the mind, seeing the phenomena, seeing the dhamma.

280
00:23:37,882 --> 00:23:39,523
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what dhamma means, literally, phenomena.

281
00:23:40,463 --> 00:23:44,825
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, the dhamma is the teaching of the knowledge of the phenomena, of that which manifests.

282
00:23:48,147 --> 00:23:50,708
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is writing, right, right there.

283
00:23:50,908 --> 00:23:51,308
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

284
00:23:51,488 --> 00:23:52,529
[SPEAKER_02]: It cannot be anywhere else.

285
00:23:54,189 --> 00:23:56,430
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's, it's, it's learning how to see it correctly.

286
00:23:59,612 --> 00:24:01,933
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it says, eh, you are merely the knower.

287
00:24:03,604 --> 00:24:18,852
[SPEAKER_01]: In the past, because the roots of greed, hatred and delusion already existed in the mind, whenever you caught sight of the slightest pleasant or unpleasant mind object, the mind would react immediately.

288
00:24:19,693 --> 00:24:23,075
[SPEAKER_01]: You would take hold of it and have to experience either happiness or suffering.

289
00:24:23,798 --> 00:24:28,160
[SPEAKER_01]: You would be continuously indulging in states of happiness and suffering.

290
00:24:28,220 --> 00:24:33,803
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the way it is as long as the mind doesn't know itself, as long as it's not bright and illuminated.

291
00:24:34,824 --> 00:24:35,744
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind is not free.

292
00:24:36,645 --> 00:24:40,266
[SPEAKER_01]: It is influenced by whatever mind objects it experiences.

293
00:24:41,167 --> 00:24:45,989
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, it is without a refuge, unable to truly depend on itself.

294
00:24:47,010 --> 00:24:51,412
[SPEAKER_01]: You receive a pleasant mental impression and get into a good mood.

295
00:24:52,152 --> 00:24:53,213
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind forgets itself.

296
00:24:54,305 --> 00:24:58,667
[SPEAKER_01]: In contrast, the original mind is beyond good and bad.

297
00:24:58,727 --> 00:25:00,407
[SPEAKER_01]: This is the original nature of the mind.

298
00:25:01,327 --> 00:25:06,289
[SPEAKER_01]: If you feel happy over experiencing a pleasant mind-object, that is delusion.

299
00:25:07,369 --> 00:25:13,732
[SPEAKER_01]: If you feel unhappy over experiencing an unpleasant mind-object, that is delusion.

300
00:25:13,852 --> 00:25:18,313
[SPEAKER_01]: Unpleasant mind-objects make you suffer and pleasant ones make you happy.

301
00:25:18,373 --> 00:25:19,013
[SPEAKER_01]: This is the world.

302
00:25:19,974 --> 00:25:21,995
[SPEAKER_01]: Mind-objects come with the world.

303
00:25:22,635 --> 00:25:23,295
[SPEAKER_01]: They are the world.

304
00:25:24,350 --> 00:25:29,593
[SPEAKER_01]: They give rise to happiness and suffering, good and evil and everything that is subject to impermanence and uncertainty.

305
00:25:30,473 --> 00:25:34,155
[SPEAKER_01]: When you separate from the original mind, everything becomes uncertain.

306
00:25:34,855 --> 00:25:42,259
[SPEAKER_01]: There is just unending birth, death, uncertainty, apprehensiveness, suffering and hardship.

307
00:25:46,241 --> 00:25:49,302
[SPEAKER_02]: And you separate it from the original mind, you separate it from that

308
00:25:52,870 --> 00:25:54,471
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the domain of phenomena.

309
00:25:54,811 --> 00:26:03,175
[SPEAKER_02]: You stop seeing, or you don't see it as a phenomena, when you never restrain your actions in regard to your senses.

310
00:26:03,215 --> 00:26:09,238
[SPEAKER_02]: The threshold of the being you are used to is on the level of the senses and pleasure or pain that comes from it.

311
00:26:09,258 --> 00:26:15,541
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why for many people, like, they would have the implicit attitude that even their own thoughts don't really exist, are not real.

312
00:26:16,721 --> 00:26:19,883
[SPEAKER_02]: Because the expectation of reality has been proliferated so far out.

313
00:26:21,056 --> 00:26:28,301
[SPEAKER_02]: So, going back to that original mind, as Ajahn Chah says, it's nothing... some hidden reality behind all these appearances.

314
00:26:28,961 --> 00:26:31,983
[SPEAKER_02]: It's actually stopped misconceiving the appearances for what they're not.

315
00:26:33,424 --> 00:26:34,524
[SPEAKER_02]: And that is its original state.

316
00:26:34,765 --> 00:26:35,505
[SPEAKER_02]: It was always there.

317
00:26:35,985 --> 00:26:38,067
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why Arahantship is possible in the first place.

318
00:26:38,727 --> 00:26:41,909
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why undoing of the wrong conceiving is possible.

319
00:26:42,890 --> 00:26:46,432
[SPEAKER_02]: Because these things are truly independent of whether you conceive them or not.

320
00:26:49,946 --> 00:26:51,627
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why sense of self is a problem.

321
00:26:52,387 --> 00:26:53,768
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's a contradiction in terms.

322
00:26:54,688 --> 00:26:58,550
[SPEAKER_02]: Self means mastery, means ownership, means rulership of your experience.

323
00:26:59,210 --> 00:27:01,651
[SPEAKER_02]: Yet, you can only rule that which was given to you beforehand.

324
00:27:03,612 --> 00:27:04,973
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means, well, you're not a ruler then.

325
00:27:06,193 --> 00:27:11,715
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if you were the true creator, master, ruler of these things, you would have been created and you would have been truly independent of it.

326
00:27:12,196 --> 00:27:17,478
[SPEAKER_02]: But your whole existence depends on this thing still being there, so that you can maintain your ownership in regard to it.

327
00:27:18,630 --> 00:27:30,921
[SPEAKER_02]: But when that thing decides to go, and it will, that's what we encourage reflecting of four great elements, how they change, you realize it's inconceivable to even imagine that you'll still exist in your domain of ownership.

328
00:27:31,561 --> 00:27:32,722
[SPEAKER_02]: So that's not ownership then.

329
00:27:33,543 --> 00:27:39,108
[SPEAKER_02]: And you realize the only way to maintain that sense of ownership of things around you is to ignore the fact that you cannot actually own it.

330
00:27:39,693 --> 00:27:42,195
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why people don't want to think about death naturally.

331
00:27:42,735 --> 00:27:49,080
[SPEAKER_02]: Don't want to think about losing the loved ones, losing things they care, because it's implicit that it will happen.

332
00:27:49,140 --> 00:27:50,901
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it just reminds them of the obvious.

333
00:27:54,204 --> 00:27:58,347
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you stop being ignorant by making effort, basically, to not ignore things.

334
00:27:58,707 --> 00:27:59,068
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

335
00:27:59,708 --> 00:28:01,469
[SPEAKER_02]: Because ignoring things, it's effortless.

336
00:28:01,830 --> 00:28:04,151
[SPEAKER_02]: It's with the grain of sensuality, with the grain of ignorance.

337
00:28:06,153 --> 00:28:07,454
[SPEAKER_02]: Not ignoring takes effort.

338
00:28:07,993 --> 00:28:13,255
[SPEAKER_02]: But not ignoring is not like, oh, I need to, like, resolve some universe mysteries.

339
00:28:13,315 --> 00:28:13,435
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

340
00:28:13,575 --> 00:28:15,996
[SPEAKER_02]: I just need to stop ignoring the very things that are in front of me.

341
00:28:16,996 --> 00:28:18,657
[SPEAKER_02]: Stop ignoring the broader context.

342
00:28:20,697 --> 00:28:22,598
[SPEAKER_02]: Stop ignoring the peripheral to my actual.

343
00:28:23,178 --> 00:28:27,119
[SPEAKER_02]: Stop trying to get rid of the states of mind I don't like.

344
00:28:27,219 --> 00:28:29,000
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, this should have not arisen, should have not arisen.

345
00:28:29,958 --> 00:28:32,120
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can look at just mind objects.

346
00:28:32,161 --> 00:28:33,782
[SPEAKER_01]: You can just mind objects.

347
00:28:33,943 --> 00:28:38,708
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what we do, like when I say, when we do the questioning, ask yourself, how am I feeling right now, fundamentally?

348
00:28:38,748 --> 00:28:39,269
[SPEAKER_02]: Is it okay?

349
00:28:39,289 --> 00:28:39,989
[SPEAKER_02]: Is it not okay?

350
00:28:40,670 --> 00:28:44,535
[SPEAKER_02]: And you realize, you find that almost as a state, that's there enduring.

351
00:28:44,835 --> 00:28:45,936
[SPEAKER_02]: And you had no say in it.

352
00:28:46,757 --> 00:28:49,160
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah, you have lots of joy now, because you feel okay.

353
00:28:49,703 --> 00:28:53,646
[SPEAKER_02]: Or you have a bit of a bit of a pressure and an unpleasant feeling because you don't feel okay.

354
00:28:54,106 --> 00:28:58,329
[SPEAKER_02]: But that fundamental bit of whether it's okay or not okay, it has a reason on its own.

355
00:28:58,750 --> 00:29:00,871
[SPEAKER_02]: Feeling has been manifested to its own extent.

356
00:29:01,412 --> 00:29:02,352
[SPEAKER_02]: And you have no say in that.

357
00:29:03,533 --> 00:29:07,376
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why I compare it to like the weather that comes and goes.

358
00:29:07,396 --> 00:29:09,557
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, you will act differently when the weather's bad.

359
00:29:09,618 --> 00:29:11,059
[SPEAKER_02]: You'll act differently when the weather's good.

360
00:29:11,139 --> 00:29:13,821
[SPEAKER_02]: You'll act differently when there's a pleasant feeling, there's unpleasant feeling.

361
00:29:14,621 --> 00:29:18,224
[SPEAKER_02]: But that in itself is not necessarily the problem until your actions

362
00:29:19,624 --> 00:29:22,765
[SPEAKER_02]: They delude themselves in believing that they are the controller of the weather.

363
00:29:23,686 --> 00:29:25,306
[SPEAKER_02]: They are the controller of the feelings.

364
00:29:26,487 --> 00:29:28,648
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's what the Buddha would ask that person in that sutta.

365
00:29:28,668 --> 00:29:33,109
[SPEAKER_02]: He says, well, if the feeling is truly yours, as in you're the controller, which one is it then?

366
00:29:34,590 --> 00:29:36,090
[SPEAKER_02]: Because they keep coming and going.

367
00:29:36,791 --> 00:29:38,731
[SPEAKER_02]: Good feelings, bad feelings, neutral feelings.

368
00:29:38,771 --> 00:29:45,174
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you were truly the owner, you would only have good feelings, because ownership and pleasure, hand in hand.

369
00:29:47,100 --> 00:29:48,761
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why you want to undermine that pleasure.

370
00:29:48,821 --> 00:29:50,543
[SPEAKER_02]: Not by, oh, I must get rid of it.

371
00:29:50,563 --> 00:29:54,565
[SPEAKER_02]: But by seeing it, that actually, it cannot be yours.

372
00:29:54,826 --> 00:29:55,886
[SPEAKER_02]: It hasn't come from you.

373
00:29:56,727 --> 00:29:58,929
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how you also then undermine the ownership.

374
00:30:01,370 --> 00:30:02,491
[SPEAKER_01]: And the seeking of it.

375
00:30:02,972 --> 00:30:03,512
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah, of course.

376
00:30:03,552 --> 00:30:04,253
[SPEAKER_02]: And then everything else.

377
00:30:04,453 --> 00:30:10,057
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, the seeking and everything else that comes, it's if you don't then make the... if you don't make the effort to undo it.

